G5207.def:
υἱός, -οῦ, ὁ, [in LXX very frequently and nearly always for בֵּן, Genèse 4:17, al.; for בַּר, Dan LXX TH 7:13, al.; etc. ;], 
a son; 
__1. in the ordinary sense: Matthieu 10:37, Marc 9:17, Luc 1:13, al. mult.; omitted with the art. of origin (WM, §30, 3; Bl., §35, 2), τὸν τοῦ Ἰεσσαί, Actes 13:22 (LXX); also with genitive anarth. (cl.), Σώπατρος Πύρρου Βεροιαῖος, Actes 20:4; with adj., προτότοκος, Luc 2:7; μονογένης, Luc 7:12; opposite to νόθος, Hébreux 12:8; in a wider sense, of posterity: ὁ υἱ. Δαυΐδ, of the Messiah (cf. Dalman, Words, 316ff.; DCG, ii, 653f.), Matthieu 22:42, 45 Marc 12:35, 37 Luc 20:41, 44 al.; υἱοὶ Ἰσραήλ, (cf. υἷες Ἀχαιῶν, Hom., Il., i, 162, al.), Matthieu 27:9, Actes 9:15, al. 
__2. Metaphorical; 
__(a) as belonging to, being connected with or having the quality of that which follows (a usage mainly due to translation from a Semitic original; cf. Deiss., BS, 161ff.; Dalman, Words, 115f.; DCG, ii, 652f.): τ. πονεροῦ (διαβόλου), Matthieu 13:38, Actes 13:10; τ. νυμφῶνος (see: νυμφών), Matthieu 9:15, Marc 2:19, al.; τ. φωτός (Lft., Notes, 74), Luc 16:8, Jean 12:36, 1 Thessaloniciens 5:5; τ. εἰρεήμης, Luc 10:6; γεέννης, Matthieu 23:15; τ. ἀπωλείας, Jean 17:12, 2 Thessaloniciens 2:3; τ. αἰῶνος τούτου, Luc 16:8 20:34; τ. ἀπειθειάς, Éphésiens 2:2 5:6; βροντῆς, Marc 3:17; τ. ἀναστάσεως, Luc 20:36; παρακλήσεως, Actes 4:36; τ. προφητῶν κ. τ. διαθήκης, Actes 3:25; 
__(b) υἱὸς τ. θεοῦ (cf. Dalman, Words, 268ff.; Deiss., BS, 166f.; DB, iv, 570 ff.; DCG, ii, 654ff.), of men, as partakers of the Divine nature and of the life to come: Matthieu 5:9, Luc 20:36, Romains 8:14 9:26, al.; υἱοὶ (κ. θυγατέρες) τ. ὑψίστου, Luc 6:35, 2 Corinthiens 6:18; in an unique sense of Jesus, Matthieu 4:3 8:29 28:19, Marc 3:4, Luc 4:41, Jean 9:35 11:27, al.; ὁ Χριστὸς ὁ υἱ. τ. θεοῦ ζῶντος (τ. εὐλογητοῦ), Matthieu 16:16, Marc 14:61; 
__(with) (ὁ) υἱὸς τοῦ ἀνθρώπου (in LXX for Heb. בּן אדם, Aram, בּר אנשׁ; cf. Dalman, Words, 234ff.; DB, iv, 579ff.; DCG, ii, 659ff.; Westc., St. John, i, 74ff.; other reff. in Swete, Mk, 2:10), based on the Aram. of Daniel 7:13, where the phrase, like the corresponding Heb. (as in Psaumes 8:5), means a man, one of the species, and indicates the human appearance of the person in question. It is used of the Messiah in Enoch, with 46, §1-4, also in II Esther 13:3, 12, al. Our Lord first makes the phrase a title, using the def. art. It seems to combine the ideas of his true humanity and representative character. Exc. in Actes 7:56 and (anarth.) Apocalypse 1:13 14:14, it is used of Jesus only by himself: Matthieu 8:20, Marc 2:10, Luc 5:24, Jean 1:52, al.

G5215.def:
ὕμνος, -ου, ὁ 
[in LXX for תְּהִלָּה, נְגִינָה, שִׁיר, etc. ;] 
a hymn; 
__(a) in cl. a festal song in praise of gods or heroes; 
__(b) in LXX and NT a song of praise addressed to God: Éphésiens 5:19, Colossiens 3:16.†
SYN.: ψαλμός, that which is sung to a musical accompaniment; ᾠδή, the generic term for song (cf. Tr., Syn., § LXXviii, Lft. on Colossiens 3:16)

G5244.def:
ὑπερήφανος, -ον
(ὑπέρ, φαίνομαι, with η pleonast., see Kühner3, I, 189) [in LXX for זֵד, גֵּאֶה, לוּץ, etc. ;] 
showing oneself above others; 
__(a) in good sense (Plat., al.), pre-eminent, splendid; 
__(b) more frequently in had sense, and so always in Scr., arrogant, haughty, disdainful (see Westc. Epp. Jo., 65b): Romains 1:30, 2 Timothée 3:2; διανοίᾳ καρδίας, Luc 1:51; opposite to ταπεινός (as in Proverbes 3:34), Jacques 4:6, 1 Pierre 5:5" (LXX) .†
SYN.: ἀλαζών, ὑβριστής, see Tr., Syn., § xxix

G5260.def:
ὑπο-βάλλω 
[in LXX: ??Daniel 3:9 A, I Esther 2:18 * ;] 
to throw or put under. Metaphorical, 
__(a) to subject, submit; 
__(b) to suggest, whisper, prompt; 
__(with) to suborn (see Field, Notes, 113), instigate: with accusative of person(s), Actes 6:11.†

G5265.def:
ὑπο-δέω 
[in LXX: 2 Chroniques 28:15, Eze.16:10 (נָעַל) * ;] 
to blind under, esp. of foot gear. Most frequently in mid, and pass. with accusative; 
__(a) of the foot, ὑποδησάμενοι τ. πόδας, your feet shod: Éphésiens 6:15; 
__(b) of that which is put on, σανδάλια: Marc 6:9, Actes 12:8.†
